schema: concept-lineage
object:
  title: dMRV (Digital Measurement, Reporting, and Verification)
  type: concept-lineage
  short_description: >-
    A system for turning real-world actions (e.g. planting a tree) into
    unchangeable, verifiable proof by recording photos, location, and other
    data on the blockchain — used to attract funding, demonstrate
    accountability, and prove contributions.
  source_traditions:
    - >-
      Named directly in the article as the term for programs like Silvi that
      prove tree planting and pay contributors for verified impact.
  toolkit_usage: >-
    Presented as the third of three Ethereum use cases relevant to "Sarreya"
    program participants: photograph a planted tree, record its location, and
    submit through an app, creating permanent, verifiable proof of the
    contribution.
  adjacent_meanings:
    - oracle (dMRV data must ultimately be trusted/fed onto the chain by someone)
  important_distinctions:
    - >-
      dMRV proves that specific reported data was submitted and recorded
      immutably; it does not, on its own, independently verify the underlying
      real-world claim (e.g. that the tree survives) — this distinction is not
      made explicit in this particular article, unlike the more cautious
      framing given in "What Web3 Can and Can't Do."
  tensions:
    - >-
      This article presents dMRV/on-chain impact proof relatively uncritically
      compared to the toolkit's own "what-web3-can-cant-do" article, which
      names MRV/carbon claims as a high-risk area requiring oracles and human
      trust, not just recording data on-chain.
  risks_of_flattening: >-
    Describing on-chain impact records as simply "permanent proof of your
    contribution" risks overstating what dMRV can guarantee — permanence of
    the record is not the same as verification of the underlying real-world
    fact.
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/what-you-can-do-ethereum.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted
